	'M3-E4':
		"Water's soft shores read the scene depth on every path: the `water-shores` picture on WebGPU, compatibility mode and WebGL2, in both reference sets, shows the pale band and foam where the water meets the sand and the island's base. The picture is a witness for the binding, and the control run proves it on the real-GPU reference set: with the blank texel bound in place of the copy, every thread mode differs from its reference at 5.163 per cent of the pixels on compatibility mode, 5.286 on WebGPU and 5.552 on WebGL2, against the 0.100 per cent limit, and the sheet draws its deep colour everywhere with no foam; with the copy bound, all three tiers pass. Both frame builders take that group from the frame graph, and the cross-path test in `crates/null3d-render/tests/custom_materials.rs` fails on either builder that picks the wrong one. Neither the software reference set's control nor a device run is owed: the group choice is engine code that every path shares, so the picture's measured dependence on the copy carries to that set and to a device. That page is a test sheet; the first user on the branch is the splats add-on's depth mixing. D-202 names M3-E4a for the case of depth beside textures",
	'M3-E4a':
		"A material that reads the scene's depth may now carry textures of its own. The frame group holds eight sampled textures and the maps' group eight, which is WebGPU's limit of sixteen in a fragment stage, so a depth-reading material could take none; it now takes a maps' group with the last slot and the displacement map left out, under a layout of its own, numbering its textures from the group's first binding and its samplers from the slot count. That keeps the frame group's eight, the maps' seven and the copy within the sixteen that every device allows, and such a material declares up to seven textures. `tests/image/manifest.ts` draws `depth-textures` in every thread mode on both paths: a beach and a pond whose water reads the depth copy beside a ripple sheet and a foam field, so a path that drops either the depth or the textures draws a different image. Three references stand in each set, and the water add-on's mirror and foam maps are its first users.",
