	'M3-R40':
		"Three places that disagreed with the engine now say what it does. The shadows concept says the core keeps only the first directional light, so a further one lights nothing and casts none, which `crates/null3d-core/src/lights.rs` bears out. The render reference's sample and the develop skill's recipe pointed their sky sun away from their own directional light; both now give a position that points toward the sun, the way that light travels, and the cookbook's sun sample and the physical-sky samples in the scene reference already mirrored theirs. The physics guide's frame loop passes two objects made once to `translation(target)` and `rotation(target)`, so it allocates nothing a frame, and its install line asks for rapier 0.20.0 or later, the first version that fills a target. The complete sketches' check, `tests/lib/skill-sketches.test.ts`, passed 5 of 5 on the merged tree. `tests/lib/physics-guide.test.ts` holds every `translation()` and `rotation()` call in the guide to a target that the setup makes once, since the sample's own libraries are not in this repository, so a read that allocated a frame would fail it.",
	'M3-R39':
		"A terrain block's bounds sat on its low corner, with a radius to its farthest vertex, and every cell crossing gave each reused block new bounds, which the performance guide counts as a draw-table rebuild. The bounds now sit on the block's middle — half its size across, the middle of the ground's own height range — and a level's sphere is therefore the same for every block of that level, so a reused block keeps them: pooling the parked meshes by level means a block only takes a mesh of its own level, and only a newly built block is given bounds. `packages/terrain/src/terrain.test.ts` holds four things: each sphere contains every vertex its mesh was made with, is centred on the block's middle, every block of a level reports the same radius, no radius is larger than that level's ground requires, and a cell crossing gives no block that already draws new bounds. For a block whose level's ground rises well above it, the level's sphere is larger than that block's own tight sphere, because one sphere holds every block of the level; that is the trade the pooling makes, and it is centred and constant, which is what removes the rebuild. The six terrain and demo-terrain pictures pass unchanged on WebGPU, compatibility mode and WebGL2, and `bench:allocation --terrain` still reads 0.0 bytes per frame at every terrain place. The demo shows the consequence in the frame counters. `tests/pages/demo-frames.html?demo=terrain`, on the WebGPU path at the High preset in the threaded, pipelined mode, settled for 135 s, one full circuit of the demo's camera, which takes 126 s, then measured twelve 5 s windows with `engine.measure()`. The sketch reported 47 finest-cell crossings, 3 to 5 in each window, and `FrameMetrics.rebuilds` read 0 in every window, with no new block built and draw calls at 1470 a frame. The same page's control read 1 rebuild for an object made during a measured window and 1 for its removal, so the counter counts a structure change. With the old per-block bounds write put back on the reused blocks, the same run read 78 rebuild frames across the same 47 crossings, so the counter moves when a crossing writes bounds and the change removes it. A shorter settle leaves a few new block builds as the per-level pool grows, and those frames read as rebuilds, so the figures need the full circuit. Its rule-42 bullet is met with M3-F14: the block build and the tree walk run in the add-on's WebAssembly module, and the package build leaves the TypeScript writer and walk out of the tarball. The terrain and demo-terrain pictures pass unchanged on the three paths, 6 of 6.",
